## 3. Standard structure

```text
store-assets/
├── store-assets-workflow.md      # process spec
├── README.md                     # static package overview
├── {{RULEBOOK_FILE}}             # this document
├── templates/                    # package-level template sources
│   └── store-rulebook.md.template
├── {{STORE_PLATFORM}}/
│   ├── current/                  # live published assets, per locale
│   ├── release-prep/             # draft assets, per locale
│   ├── submissions/              # one record file per submission
│   └── templates/                # store-specific record template sources
├── apple-app-store/              # reserved skeleton, future Adapter
│   ├── current/
│   ├── release-prep/
│   ├── submissions/
│   └── templates/
└── google-play/                  # reserved skeleton, future Adapter
    ├── current/
    ├── release-prep/
    ├── submissions/
    └── templates/
```

The layout is identical across store adapters by design; the lifecycle is shared, but each adapter keeps its own fields, templates, and validation rules. `apple-app-store/` and `google-play/` exist as **empty reserved skeletons** only — no rules for them are assumed until researched and verified.

`current/`, `release-prep/`, and `submissions/` are created by first initialization; locale subfolders are created when a locale is actually used.

## 4. Directory responsibilities

### `current/`

Represents the live store state, organized per locale:

```text
current/<locale>/
├── listing.md
├── release-notes.md
└── screenshots/
    ├── source/
    └── export/
```

- Must not be edited while preparing an update.
- Must not be replaced until the user confirms the new submission is live.
- Older published versions are preserved through Git history.

### `release-prep/`

Contains draft assets for an upcoming submission, per locale, mirroring `current/<locale>/` so publish is a tree-level move.

```text
release-prep/<locale>/
├── listing.md
├── release-notes.md
└── screenshots/
    ├── source/
    └── export/
```

- Prepare updates only here, never in `current/`.
- The submission variables live in the submission record (in `submissions/`).
- The status field tracks the whole flow; no per-folder naming needed.
- Use the same tree whether the update is a new build or only listing metadata and screenshots.

### `submissions/`

One record file per submission:

```text
submissions/<submission-id>.md
```

Records submission ID, date, application version, update type, locales, status, publication date, changes, submitted files, and review notes. Git history preserves exact versions, so records do not duplicate all assets.

### `templates/`

Two template locations, kept apart on purpose:

- `templates/` (package level) — the template source that generates this rulebook. The package `README.md` is static, not generated.
- `<STORE_PLATFORM>/templates/` — store-specific record templates (listing, release notes, submission records), reserved for a later phase.

Use an existing template instead of inventing a new structure.

## 5. Naming rules

- Directories: lowercase `kebab-case` (`store-assets`, `microsoft-store`, `release-prep`); locales use official codes (`en-US`, `zh-CN`).
- Markdown files: short lowercase `kebab-case` names (`listing.md`, `release-notes.md`, `2026-08-04-listing-refresh.md`).
- Every level-1 heading includes the product name:
  - `# {{PRODUCT_NAME}} Microsoft Store Listing — {{LOCALE}}`
  - `# {{PRODUCT_NAME}} Microsoft Store Release Notes — {{LOCALE}}`
  - `# {{PRODUCT_NAME}} Microsoft Store Submission — {{SUBMISSION_ID}}`
- Screenshots: two-digit order + semantic lowercase `kebab-case`, no spaces, no version words (`final`, `new`, `latest`):

  ```text
  01-primary-value.png
  02-core-workflow.png
  ```

  Editable files go in `screenshots/<locale>/source/`; upload-ready files in `screenshots/<locale>/export/`.

